---
description: Since C# 5.0, async and await are contextual keywords. Contextual keywords do have a particular meaning in some contexts, but are not reserved and therefore can be used as variable names.
globs:
  - '**/*.cs'
alwaysApply: true
---
# Rule content

<pre data-diff-id="1" data-diff-type="noncompliant">
int await = 42; // Noncompliant, but compiles
int async = 42; // Noncompliant, but compiles
</pre>
 Keywords, on the other hand, are always reserved and therefore are not valid variable names. <pre>
int abstract = 42; // Error CS1585: Member modifier 'abstract' must precede the member type and name
int foreach = 42; // Error CS1519: Invalid token 'foreach' in class, struct, or interface member declaration
</pre>
 To avoid any confusion, it is best to not use async and await as identifiers. <pre data-diff-id="1" data-diff-type="compliant">
int someVariableName = 42;
int someOtherVariableName = 42;
</pre>
